SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING
Issue 304 | Wednesday, March 4, 2026

Good morning.

1) Air cargo yields fall 12% as e-commerce volumes normalize
The transpacific air market had two years of e-commerce parcels filling freighters at premium rates. That is ending: the de minimis changes cut the parcel flow, and the freighters that were pulled into the lane are now chasing general cargo. Forwarders say spot rates from Hong Kong to Chicago are back at 2019 levels in real terms.

2) CBP finalizes the enhanced entry data requirement
From July 1, low-value shipments must be filed with a ten-digit HTS code, the seller's identity, and the URL of the product listing. The agency says it is about enforcement; the express carriers say it is about $2 per parcel in filing cost. Both are right.

3) Almond bloom is early and heavy
Central Valley growers report the strongest bloom since 2021, helped by a warm February. Good news for the crop, bad news for anyone booking reefer capacity out of Oakland in September, when it all has to move. Book early; we say this every year and every year someone does not.

NUMBER OF THE DAY: 3.1 days. Median dwell time for import containers at the Port of New York and New Jersey in February, the best since the Commission started publishing it.

That's the morning. Reply with tips.

